<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mixtecs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ñuu Savi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mixtecs, who call themselves Ñuu Savi, 'people of the rain', are an indigenous people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> and Puebla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Before the Spanish conquest they lived in many small kingdoms such as Tilantongo and Tututepec, whose histories they recorded in painted screenfold books, and were renowned for gold work and turquoise mosaic. Many fell under Aztec rule in the 15th century. Mixtec languages are still widely spoken.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mixtecs.jpg" alt="Mixtecs" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [2] · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jarabe_Mixteco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The law distinguished a republic of Spaniards from a republic of Indians, whose towns kept their own councils and nobles, and Nahuatl, <b>Mixtec</b> and other languages were used in local records.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Coixtlahuaca developed in a valley of the Mixteca Alta inhabited by Chocho (Ngiwa) and <b>Mixtec</b> people.</blockquote><a href="/state/coixtlahuaca">Coixtlahuaca</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitla served as a religious centre and was linked politically with the Zapotec kingdom of Zaachila, while sharing <b>Mixtec</b> artistic influences.</blockquote><a href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mixtec</b> nobles later reused its tombs, including the famous Tomb 7 treasure.</blockquote><a href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a></li><li><blockquote>Teozacoalco, Chiyo Cahnu in <b>Mixtec</b>, was a royal seat in the western Mixteca Alta by about 1000.</blockquote><a href="/state/teozacoalco">Teozacoalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Tilantongo, Ñuu Tnoo in <b>Mixtec</b>, emerged as a royal seat in the Mixteca Alta by the 10th century, around 900.</blockquote><a href="/state/tilantongo">Tilantongo</a></li></ul></section>
